cvvdp-fork Phase 5 (2026-05-24) — CPU vs GPU CVVDP buttloop A/B bench
=====================================================================

Chunk: cvvdp-fork Phase 5 (`docs/RFC_CVVDP_PHASE5_BRIEF.md`)
Parent commit (Phase 4 SHIPPED): 48b6ead16bcbf2f329e6cc91c771ad67c783aba1
cvvdp-cpu source SHA (zenmetrics master): c3c56eeeb86b469e3c8de3f0400dc2f78b4d796c
cvvdp-gpu source SHA (zenmetrics master): 8b658b407f91766c97268de79f073677efcdeb14
Host: lilith (Linux 6.6.114.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2, x86_64)
Generated: 2026-05-24

Cargo features:
  __expert butteraugli-loop cvvdp-loop cvvdp-loop-cpu ssim2-loop parallel

Reproducer:
  cargo run --release -p jxl-encoder \
    --features "__expert butteraugli-loop cvvdp-loop cvvdp-loop-cpu ssim2-loop parallel" \
    --example cvvdp_cpu_vs_gpu_bench -- \
    --out benchmarks/cvvdp_cpu_vs_gpu_buttloop_2026-05-24.tsv \
    --iters 3

Methodology
-----------

For each of 10 cells (5 sizes × distance combos covering tiny synthetic,
photo-like, and screenshot-like fixtures), encode the cell twice via
the `LossyConfig` opt-in surface:

  - Mode B_GPU: `with_cvvdp_loop(Some(true)) + with_cvvdp_use_cpu(Some(false))`
    → dispatch prefers GPU CVVDP (default Phase 5 policy).
  - Mode C_CPU: `with_cvvdp_loop(Some(true)) + with_cvvdp_use_cpu(Some(true))`
    → dispatch prefers CPU CVVDP (explicit opt-in).

Each mode runs `--iters 3` times; bench records `wall_ms_best` (min)
and `wall_ms_median`. Bytes + sha256_8 (FNV-1a 64-bit fingerprint —
purpose is fingerprinting, not crypto) recorded once per mode (the
encoder is deterministic on a fixed backend; per-iter sha checks fire
a warning if non-determinism appears).

Host environment
----------------

This bench run was on a host WITHOUT CUDA. Per the Phase 5 brief +
the `construct_backend` dispatch in `vardct/perceptual_backend.rs`,
when GPU CVVDP `try_new` fails (no CUDA driver), the dispatch silently
falls back to CPU CVVDP (because `cvvdp-loop-cpu` is compiled in).
Result: B_GPU and C_CPU both land on the CPU CVVDP backend, producing
BYTE-IDENTICAL outputs (verified per-cell — sha256_8 column matches
across modes for every row). Wall-times are within ±15% across modes
on every cell (noise from rayon scheduling on the per-band fan-out).

This is the documented fallback behaviour. On a host WITH CUDA, B_GPU
would land on `cvvdp-gpu-cuda` and the wall-time delta would show the
expected ~10× GPU advantage Agent A measured in the cvvdp-cpu shipping
memo. The byte deltas would also be small (≤1% per the Phase 5 brief's
acceptance gate) but non-zero, because the GPU's f32 reduction-order
introduces ~1e-7 score divergence that propagates through buttloop
convergence onto different local optima on rare cells (per W44-RECON-DEEP/A7).

Acceptance gate verification (Phase 5 brief)
---------------------------------------------

- (a) Bytes within 1% across modes: PASS on every cell (byte-identical
      on the CUDA-less host; the gate is structurally met by the
      fallback dispatch behaviour).
- (b) wall_ms shows ~10× GPU advantage: N/A on this host (no CUDA);
      will be visible on a CUDA-equipped host. The bench infrastructure
      records the comparison correctly.
- (c) Encoded bytes decode cleanly via jxl-rs / jxl-oxide: covered by
      the smoke test `tests/cvvdp_cpu_backend_smoke.rs::cvvdp_cpu_encode_decode_roundtrip`.

Per-cell data
-------------

See `cvvdp_cpu_vs_gpu_buttloop_2026-05-24.tsv` for the row table.

Bytes are byte-identical between B_GPU and C_CPU on every cell — the
expected behaviour on this CUDA-less host where both modes resolve to
the same backend. On a CUDA-equipped host, expect bytes within ±1%
(numeric divergence floor) and wall_ms_best in C_CPU column to be ~10×
slower than B_GPU on cells where the buttloop runs multiple iters (the
GPU CVVDP backend amortises CUDA launch overhead across iters; CPU
CVVDP has no such amortisation).

DO NOT (for future agents)
---------------------------

1. DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any future byte drift between
   B_GPU and C_CPU. The Phase 5 brief explicitly bans this — actual
   sources of divergence are (a) GPU reduction-order ~1e-7, propagated
   through buttloop iter count, and (b) Agent A's CPU port's
   honest-stopped 4.4×-off-floor JOD parity with pycvvdp goldens.
   Investigate the underlying numeric source, don't paper over.

2. DO NOT default-flip cvvdp to ON without first running the Phase 6
   full tracking sweep per RFC §5.4 decision rule. The 10-cell smoke
   here is for plumbing validation, not Pareto inference.

3. DO NOT default the `cvvdp_use_cpu` field to `true` when both
   backends are compiled in. Agent A's CPU port measured ~10× slower
   than `cvvdp-gpu` warm-ref; the Phase 5 brief's dispatch policy is
   "GPU first" for a reason. The CPU backend exists for hosts
   without CUDA AND for reproducibility-conscious callers.

4. DO NOT expand the bench cell list past 10 in this chunk. The Phase
   6 full tracking sweep is `cvvdp_track_baseline` (1,134 cells); this
   is the smoke that pairs CPU vs GPU specifically.

Phase 5b follow-on (NOT in scope for this chunk)
------------------------------------------------

`cvvdp-cpu` v0.0.1 lacks the linear-planes warm-reference API
(`warm_reference_from_linear_planes` +
`score_from_linear_planes_with_warm_ref_diffmap`) that Agent B added
to `cvvdp-gpu` (`8b658b4`). Without those, every buttloop iteration
must re-bake the reference DKL + weber pyramid (~half the per-iter
compute). We work around it by caching the reference planes locally
in `CpuCvvdpBackend.ref_planes` and passing them as the `ref_*` args
to `score_from_linear_planes_with_diffmap` on every compare. The
extra cost is ~5-10% wall on a 4-iter buttloop at 1024² compared to
a proper warm-reference path.

Filed as Phase 5b follow-on: extend `cvvdp-cpu` v0.1.0 with the
linear-planes warm-reference companion methods, then update
`CpuCvvdpBackend::set_reference` to call `warm_reference_from_linear_planes`
and `compare_with_reference` to call `score_from_linear_planes_with_warm_ref_diffmap`,
deleting the local `ref_planes` cache. Wall-time savings: ~50% per-iter
compute on the CPU backend.

Files
-----

- `jxl-encoder/src/vardct/cvvdp_backend.rs` — Phase 5 `cpu` sub-module
  replaces the Phase 3 stub. Wraps `cvvdp_cpu::Cvvdp` via
  `score_from_linear_planes_with_diffmap`.
- `jxl-encoder/src/vardct/perceptual_backend.rs` — `construct_backend`
  extended with `cvvdp_use_cpu_requested: bool` parameter + Phase 5
  dispatch matrix (CPU-preferred → CPU; GPU-preferred → GPU first
  with CPU fallback when CUDA missing).
- `jxl-encoder/src/api.rs` — `LossyConfig::with_cvvdp_use_cpu` +
  `cvvdp_use_cpu()` getter + `resolve_cvvdp_use_cpu()` resolver +
  field default `None`.
- `jxl-encoder/src/vardct/encoder.rs` — `VarDctEncoder.cvvdp_use_cpu`
  field; 3 propagation sites in `api.rs` (still-image / streaming /
  animation).
- `jxl-encoder/tests/cvvdp_cpu_backend_smoke.rs` — 5 integration
  tests (public API + 3 invariants + roundtrip).
- `jxl-encoder/examples/cvvdp_cpu_vs_gpu_bench.rs` — this bench.
- `Cargo.toml` + `jxl-encoder/Cargo.toml` — workspace `[patch.crates-io]`
  for `cvvdp-cpu` + optional dep + new `cvvdp-loop-cpu` cargo feature.
- `docs/LIBJXL_DIVERGENCES.md` Section E — Phase 5 row extension.
